    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "       \n   To determine the ratio of the volume of a cube with an edge length of six inches to the volume of a cube with an edge length of one foot, we first need to calculate the volume of each cube.\n\n1. **Volume of a cube**:\n   The volume \\(V\\) of a cube with edge length \\(a\\) is given by:\n   \\[\n   V = a^3\n   \\]\n\n2. **Volume of the first cube**:\n   Here, the edge length \\(a\\) is 6 inches.\n   \\[\n   V_1 = 6^3 = 6 \\times 6 \\times 6 = 216 \\, \\text{cubic inches}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Volume of the second cube**:\n   Here, the edge length \\(a\\) is 1 foot. We need to convert 1 foot to inches (since our units for the first cube are inches).\n   \\[\n   1 \\, \\text{foot} = 12 \\, \\text{inches}\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the volume of the second cube is:\n   \\[\n   V_2 = 12^3 = 12 \\times 12 \\times 12 = 1728 \\, \\text{cubic inches}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Ratio calculation**:\n   The ratio of the volume of the first cube to the volume of the second cube is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Ratio} = \\frac{V_1}{V_2} = \\frac{216}{1728}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Simplification**:\n   To simplify the fraction \\(\\frac{216}{1728}\\), we can find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 216 and 1728. Using the Euclidean algorithm:\n\n   - \\( 1728 \\div 216 = 7 \\) with a remainder of \\( 72 \\)\n   \\[\n   1728 = 216 \\times 7 + 72\n   \\]\n   - \\( 216 \\div 72 = 3 \\) with a remainder of \\( 0 \\)\n   \\[\n   216 = 72 \\times 3 + 0\n   \\]\n\n   The GCD of 216 and 1728 is 72.\n\n   - Divide both the numerator and denominator by their GCD:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{216}{1728} = \\frac{216 \\div 72}{1728 \\div 72} = \\frac{3}{24} = \\frac{1}{8}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the volume of a cube with edge length six inches to the volume of a cube with edge length one foot is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{8}}\n\\]",
